  <p><strong>Note for C-section recovery:</strong> In the first week after surgery, focus on easily digestible, warm, non-gas-forming foods. Avoid raw vegetables, heavy meats, and gas-producing foods like rajma and chana in the first 3–4 days post-surgery.</p>
  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <h2 id="toc-6">Common Myths About Breastfeeding After C-Section</h2>
  <p>Many of the fears mothers have are rooted in myths. These myths cause unnecessary distress — and sometimes cause mothers to give up before their body has had the chance to fully produce. Here is the truth:</p>
      MythC-section mothers cannot breastfeed successfully.
      FactThis is entirely false.
      <p>Millions of mothers who deliver by C-section breastfeed successfully and for extended periods. The path requires more intentionality — but the destination is entirely the same.</p>
      MythIf milk hasn't come in by Day 2, it never will.
      FactThis is a timing difference, not a failure.
      <p>Milk taking 3–5 days to come in is completely normal after a C-section. The answer is patience and persistent stimulation — not panic.</p>
      MythFormula is the only option after a difficult C-section.
      FactBreastfeeding is achievable for most mothers.
      <p>Formula may be needed as a supplement in some situations — but for most mothers, with the right support, breastfeeding after a C-section is fully achievable. A Motherly lactation consultant can guide you through supplementing safely while protecting your supply.</p>
      MythPain medication after surgery ruins your milk.
      FactMost medications are compatible with breastfeeding.
      <p>The pain medications typically used after a C-section — common NSAIDs, paracetamol, and appropriate opioids — are generally considered compatible with breastfeeding. Always confirm with your doctor, but pain medication alone is not a reason to stop.</p>
      MythSmall breasts mean less milk after a C-section.
      FactBreast size has no effect on milk production.
      <p>Milk supply is determined by glandular tissue and feed/pump frequency — not by breast size. This is as true after a C-section as for any breastfeeding mother.</p>
  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <h2 id="toc-7">When Your Baby Is in the NICU</h2>
  <p>One of the most challenging situations after a C-section is when your baby requires NICU care and cannot be with you to feed. The separation adds emotional pain to physical recovery — and makes establishing supply exponentially harder without professional support.</p>
  <p>If your baby is in the NICU, here is the approach that gives you the best chance:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Begin pumping within 1–6 hours of surgery — this is the most critical action when your baby cannot nurse</li>
      <li>Use a hospital-grade double electric pump — the most effective tool when direct nursing is not possible</li>
      <li>Pump every 2–3 hours, including once at night — maintain this rhythm even when exhausted</li>
      <li>Bring a photo or item of clothing from your baby to each pump session — the scent and image trigger oxytocin</li>
      <li>Ask the NICU team about skin-to-skin visits — even brief kangaroo care sessions dramatically support supply</li>
      <li>Ask your hospital's lactation team for daily support</li>
      <li>Bring expressed milk to the NICU — it is the most powerful medicine your baby can receive</li>
    </ul>
  <p>A Motherly lactation consultant with NICU experience can support you through this specific and challenging journey. Book through the app as early as possible.</p>
