  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2 id="toc-10">FAQs — How to Increase Breast Milk Supply After C-Section Delivery</h2>
        Why is milk supply lower after a C-section compared to vaginal birth?
        +
        How to increase breast milk supply after C-section delivery is more challenging because the hormonal cascade that triggers full milk production — particularly the oxytocin surge of labour — may not occur in the same way during a planned C-section. Additionally, IV fluids, anaesthesia, pain medications, physical separation of mother and baby, and delayed first feed all contribute to a slower start. However, with early, frequent breast stimulation and professional support, it is achievable for most mothers.
        When should I start trying to breastfeed or pump after a C-section?
        +
        The first step should begin within 1–6 hours of delivery — as soon as you are medically cleared and awake. Begin hand expression or nursing at the breast as soon as possible. If your baby is in the NICU, request a hospital-grade pump immediately. The golden window is the first 24 hours — every hour of stimulation in this period matters enormously.
        How long does it take for milk to come in after a C-section?
        +
        For most mothers after a C-section, mature milk comes in between Day 3 and Day 5 post-surgery — compared to Day 2–4 for most vaginal births. This delay is normal and not a sign of failure. Consistent feeding and pumping during this window maintains the demand signal. If milk has not come in by Day 5, contact a Motherly lactation consultant immediately.
        Can I take fenugreek (methi) to increase milk supply after a C-section?
        +
        For most healthy mothers, fenugreek is safe and one of the most effective galactagogue foods after a C-section. However, because fenugreek can interact with some post-surgical medications and blood thinners, always confirm with your doctor before using it. If cleared, soak 1 tsp overnight and drink the water each morning.
        I had an emergency C-section and my baby is in the NICU. Can I still breastfeed?
        +
        Yes — and this is one of the most important situations where support matters most. Begin pumping as soon as medically possible after surgery. Use a hospital-grade double pump every 2–3 hours. Bring your expressed milk to the NICU — it is the most beneficial thing you can provide your baby right now. Motherly's certified lactation consultants have experience with exactly this situation. Book through the app at mothrly.com.
        Does pain medication after C-section affect breast milk quality?
        +
        Most pain medications used after a C-section — common NSAIDs, paracetamol, and the opioids typically used in the first 24–48 hours — are considered compatible with breastfeeding at the doses prescribed postoperatively. They do not prevent milk supply and should not stop you from breastfeeding or pumping. Always confirm the specific medications with your doctor.
        How many times should I pump per day to increase supply after a C-section?
        +
        Aim for 8–12 pumping or nursing sessions per 24 hours, spaced approximately 2–3 hours apart. This includes at least one session between 1–5 AM when prolactin is highest. In the first week, quantity per session matters less than frequency — every session sends a demand signal to your body. Add power pumping once daily from Day 3–5 onwards for maximum impact.
        How does Motherly help mothers with breast milk supply after C-section delivery?
        +
        Motherly connects mothers with certified lactation consultants who specialise in milk supply after C-section delivery. Our consultants provide in-home or online support for hand expression, pumping technique, latch assessment, galactagogue guidance, and personalised plans. We also provide postnatal nannies so you can rest and recover — which directly supports supply by reducing cortisol. Book through the Motherly app — free on Android and iOS — or visit mothrly.com.
