  <h2 id="toc-6">What NOT to Eat When Breastfeeding</h2>
  <p>Knowing which foods can reduce supply, affect milk quality, or pose risks to your baby is just as important as knowing the right ones.</p>
    <strong>Peppermint and sage in large quantities</strong> — both contain compounds believed to reduce milk supply. Avoid peppermint tea and large quantities of sage in cooking while breastfeeding.
    <strong>Alcohol — avoid entirely</strong> — alcohol suppresses prolactin, passes directly into breast milk, and reduces both supply and quality.
    <strong>Excess caffeine — limit to 1–2 cups daily</strong> — more than 200–300 mg can dehydrate the body, reduce milk quality, and affect your baby's sleep.
    <strong>Heavily processed and junk foods</strong> — chips, packaged snacks, and instant noodles displace the nutritious foods your body needs for milk production, adding empty calories without protein, iron, or calcium.
    <strong>Extreme spices in large quantities</strong> — moderate spice is fine, but very large amounts of chilli can occasionally cause discomfort in sensitive babies. Watch your baby's response.
    <strong>Parsley in large quantities</strong> — like sage and peppermint, large amounts of parsley are associated with reduced supply. Use sparingly as a garnish, not as a primary cooking herb.
  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <h2 id="toc-7">Practical Tips for Daily Practice</h2>
    ✦<strong>Eat within 30 minutes of waking</strong>Your body needs fuel to produce milk through the morning feeding sessions. The most practical tip for mothers who skip breakfast.
    ✦<strong>Never skip meals</strong>Your body cannot produce milk from nothing. Eating consistently is as important as choosing the right foods.
    ✦<strong>Cook in iron vessels</strong>Iron leaches into food during cooking, especially in acidic tomato-based dals and curries. One of the simplest traditional ways to boost dietary iron.
    ✦<strong>Always pair iron-rich foods with Vitamin C</strong>Lemon on dal, amla juice with meals, or tomatoes in sabzi. Vitamin C triples iron absorption.
    ✦<strong>Eat fresh, warm food</strong>Traditional postpartum wisdom recommends warm, freshly cooked meals. Warm food is easier to digest and more effectively nourishing.
    ✦<strong>Prepare galactagogue snacks in advance</strong>Til laddoos, methi laddoos, date-almond balls, ragi cookies. Batch-preparing these means you always have a lactation snack within reach.
    ✦<strong>Eat ghee daily</strong>A small amount of ghee on roti or in dal adds healthy fats, supports nutrient absorption, and is a deeply traditional part of Indian postpartum nutrition.
