  <h2 id="toc-10">FAQs — What to Eat to Increase Breast Milk</h2>
        What is the single best food to eat to increase breast milk?
        +
        The most consistently recommended single food is methi (fenugreek) seeds — the most widely researched galactagogue food in the world. Soak 1 tsp overnight and eat with water in the morning. For immediate nutritional impact, moringa leaves added daily to dal or soup is the most evidence-backed choice for overall milk volume and quality. Combine both for maximum effect.
        Does eating oats really help to increase breast milk?
        +
        Yes — oats are consistently recommended by lactation experts. They are rich in iron (low iron being a common cause of reduced supply) and contain beta-glucan, a fibre linked to elevated prolactin levels. A daily bowl of oat porridge with banana, flaxseeds, and honey is a simple, evidence-based breakfast for breastfeeding mothers.
        What Indian foods should I eat daily to increase breast milk?
        +
        The most important daily lactation foods for Indian mothers are: methi seeds (soaked and eaten in the morning), moringa leaves (in dal or sabzi at every meal), garlic (in daily tadka), jeera and ajwain (in cooking and as flavoured water), ragi (in porridge, roti, or laddoos), til (sesame seeds in laddoos or chutney), masoor or moong dal (twice daily), dates (as a snack), and jaggery instead of sugar.
        How quickly will I see results from changing what I eat?
        +
        Most mothers notice an improvement within 3–7 days of consistently applying these food changes. Fenugreek and moringa can show results in 24–72 hours. Iron-rich foods take 1–2 weeks to meaningfully affect iron levels and supply. Hydration changes show results fastest — within 24 hours. If supply has not improved after one week of consistent effort, book a Motherly lactation consultant for a personalised assessment.
        Should I eat more calories while breastfeeding?
        +
        Yes — breastfeeding requires approximately 300–500 extra calories daily above your pre-pregnancy intake. Focus on nutrient-dense foods rather than calorie counting. Skipping meals or eating too little is one of the most common causes of reduced supply. Do not restrict calories while breastfeeding.
        Is it safe to eat fenugreek (methi) seeds to increase breast milk?
        +
        For most breastfeeding mothers, fenugreek is safe and effective. However, if you have thyroid issues, diabetes, or are on blood thinners, consult your doctor before using it regularly. Some babies become gassy when their mothers eat fenugreek — monitor your baby's response. Limit to 1 tsp of seeds daily.
        Can my diet affect the nutritional quality of breast milk?
        +
        Yes — your diet directly influences the vitamin, fatty acid, and trace mineral content of your breast milk. Omega-3 DHA from walnuts, flaxseeds, and fish improves your baby's brain development through milk. Vitamin D from eggs and fortified dairy reaches your baby through milk. Iron from dal and moringa supports your supply. It is not just about volume — it is about giving your baby the richest possible milk.
        How does Motherly support mothers with breastfeeding nutrition?
        +
        Motherly connects breastfeeding mothers with certified lactation consultants who provide personalised guidance on feeding frequency, latch assessment, supply planning, and galactagogue food recommendations. If diet changes alone have not resolved your supply concerns, a Motherly lactation consultant can identify underlying issues and create a complete plan. Book directly through the Motherly app — free on Android and iOS — or visit mothrly.com.
