  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <h2 id="toc-5">What NOT to Drink When Breastfeeding</h2>
  <p>Knowing which drinks actively suppress milk production, reduce milk quality, or pose risks to your baby is just as important as knowing the right ones.</p>
        <h4>Alcohol — Avoid Completely</h4>
        <p>Alcohol passes directly into breast milk and suppresses prolactin — the hormone at the heart of your supply. There is no safe amount of alcohol while breastfeeding.</p>
        <h4>Excess Caffeine — Limit to 1–2 Cups Daily</h4>
        <p>Caffeine dehydrates the body and passes into breast milk. More than 200–300 mg daily can reduce supply through dehydration and directly affect your baby's sleep.</p>
        <h4>Carbonated and Fizzy Drinks — Limit Significantly</h4>
        <p>Carbonated drinks contribute little to hydration, often contain caffeine or sugar, and displace the nutritious fluids your body needs for milk production.</p>
        <h4>Sugary Juices and Energy Drinks — Avoid</h4>
        <p>High-sugar drinks spike blood sugar and add empty calories. Energy drinks contain stimulants that can affect your baby. Both undermine milk supply.</p>
        <h4>Excess Cold Drinks — Limit, Especially Postpartum</h4>
        <p>Traditional postpartum wisdom across India cautions against excess cold drinks in the weeks after birth. Warm drinks are generally more effective for galactagogue herbs.</p>
        <h4>Peppermint and Sage Tea — Avoid During Breastfeeding</h4>
        <p>Peppermint and sage contain compounds believed to reduce milk supply. Avoid these herbal teas and check ingredients in any herbal blends you consume.</p>
  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <h2 id="toc-6">A Sample Daily Drinking Routine</h2>
  <p>Knowing what to drink is one thing. Building it into a daily routine is another. Here is a simple, practical daily drinking schedule that covers the full spectrum — from plain water to galactagogue teas to nutrient-dense warm drinks.</p>
      6:30 AM
      1 large glass of warm water with lemon and a pinch of jaggery — iron and hydration to start the day
      7:30 AM
      1 glass warm milk with saffron or cardamom — calcium and B12
      9:00 AM
      Morning feed — drink a full glass of water immediately before or during the feed
      10:30 AM
      1 cup fenugreek tea or moringa tea — galactagogue support
      12:00 PM
      1 glass warm jeera water before lunch — iron, digestion, prolactin support
      1:30 PM
      1 glass coconut water after lunch — electrolytes and hydration
      3:30 PM
      1 cup moringa tea or ajwain water — lactation and digestion support
      5:00 PM
      Evening feed — 1 glass of water before the feed
      7:00 PM
      1 bowl warm rasam or dal water with dinner — iron, protein, fluids
      9:30 PM
      1 glass warm shatavari-almond milk before bed — prolactin, calcium, recovery
  <p>This routine gives you approximately 12–14 glasses of fluid and covers the full spectrum of lactation-supporting drinks. Adapt it to your taste, your schedule, and what is available to you.</p>
  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <h2 id="toc-7">When Drinks Alone Are Not Enough — Expert Support</h2>
  <p>Knowing what to drink gives you a powerful foundation. But sometimes, low milk supply goes beyond hydration — it involves latch issues, hormonal factors, or feeding patterns that only a <a href="https://mothrly.com/our-services/lactation-consultants/" target="_blank" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;"><strong>certified lactation consultant</strong></a> can assess and address.</p>
      <h3><a href="https://mothrly.com/our-services/lactation-consultants/" target="_blank" style="color:var(--red-mid);text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Lactation Consultants</a></h3>
      <p>Personalised guidance on feeding frequency, latch correction, supply planning, and galactagogue recommendations.</p>
      <h3><a href="https://mothrly.com/our-services/doulas/" target="_blank" style="color:var(--red-mid);text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Doulas</a></h3>
      <p>Emotional and physical postpartum support to reduce stress — a key factor in milk supply.</p>
