  <h2 id="toc-8">Your Milk Supply Is Worth Fighting For</h2>
  <p>Every mother who searches for what to drink to increase breast milk is already doing the most important thing: she is showing up. She is asking questions. She is looking for natural, effective, safe answers — and that commitment is the foundation of a successful breastfeeding journey.</p>
  <p>The answer is not one drink. It is a combination — water as the foundation, galactagogue teas and traditional Indian drinks as the support, warm and nourishing fluids as the daily ritual. Combined with correct feeding technique, adequate rest, and expert guidance when needed, it becomes one of the most powerful tools you have.</p>
  What to drink to increase breast milk? The answer is in your kitchen, in your habits, and in the support of people who care about your journey.
  <hr>
  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2 id="toc-9">FAQs — What to Drink to Increase Breast Milk</h2>
        What is the single best drink to increase breast milk quickly?
        +
        The single most impactful drink is plain water. Most mothers who are mildly dehydrated see a noticeable supply improvement within 24–48 hours of increasing fluid intake to 12–14 glasses daily. For a galactagogue boost, fenugreek tea (1–2 cups daily) consistently shows the fastest results among herbal drinks, with many mothers reporting improvement within 24–72 hours. Combine both for the best immediate effect.
        How much water should I drink daily to increase breast milk?
        +
        Aim for 12–14 glasses (approximately 3–3.5 litres) of total fluid daily while breastfeeding. This includes water, milk, herbal teas, soups, and coconut water. A practical starting point: drink one full glass of water every time you sit down to breastfeed. Your body naturally triggers thirst during feeding — that is your signal to drink immediately.
        Is fenugreek tea safe to drink to increase breast milk?
        +
        For most healthy breastfeeding mothers, fenugreek tea is safe and effective. However, if you have thyroid conditions, diabetes, or are on blood thinners, consult your doctor before using fenugreek. Some babies may become gassy when their mothers drink fenugreek tea regularly — monitor your baby's response. Limit to 1–2 cups daily.
        Does warm milk really help to increase breast milk?
        +
        Yes — warm milk is one of the most nutritionally complete lactation drinks available. It provides calcium, protein, Vitamin B12, and Vitamin D, all supporting milk production and quality. The warmth also supports relaxation and the let-down reflex. Adding turmeric, saffron, or cardamom enhances its benefit further. Drink 2–3 glasses daily.
        Can I drink coconut water every day to increase breast milk?
        +
        Yes — coconut water is an excellent daily lactation drink. It provides natural electrolytes, potassium, and lauric acid, which is also found in breast milk and supports your baby's immunity. 1–2 glasses of fresh tender coconut water daily is safe and effective. If you have elevated blood sugar or gestational diabetes, consult your doctor about the appropriate quantity.
        What Indian traditional drinks are recommended to increase breast milk?
        +
        Traditional Indian postpartum culture offers some of the richest lactation drinks: jeera (cumin) water sipped throughout the day, ajwain (carom seed) water for digestion and milk stimulation, methi (fenugreek) seed tea, dal water strained from cooked lentils, warm rasam with pepper and cumin, shatavari in warm milk, and almond-milk drinks with saffron and cardamom. Most have at least preliminary scientific support for their role in supporting milk supply.
        Should I avoid any drinks when breastfeeding?
        +
        Yes. Avoid alcohol entirely — it suppresses prolactin and passes into milk. Limit caffeine to under 200 mg daily (roughly 1–2 cups of coffee). Avoid sugary commercial drinks and energy drinks. Limit carbonated beverages. Peppermint and sage teas are believed to reduce supply and should be avoided. These are the drinks that work against your supply efforts.
        How can Motherly help me with breastfeeding and increasing milk supply?
        +
        Motherly connects breastfeeding mothers with certified lactation consultants who provide personalised guidance on feeding frequency, latch assessment, supply planning, and galactagogue recommendations. If your supply has not responded to what you have tried, a Motherly lactation consultant can identify the root cause and create a complete plan. Book directly through the Motherly app — free on Android and iOS — or visit mothrly.com.
