  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2 id="toc-10">FAQs — When Does Breast Milk Start During Pregnancy?</h2>
        Which month does breast milk start during pregnancy exactly?
        +
        Breast milk in the form of colostrum begins from Month 4 to Month 5 — approximately Weeks 16 to 22. Some mothers begin colostrum production as early as Month 3 (Week 12), while others do not notice it until later in the second trimester. By the time your baby is born, colostrum is ready — regardless of whether you noticed it during pregnancy.
        Is it normal to leak breast milk during pregnancy?
        +
        Yes, leaking colostrum during pregnancy is completely normal and typically begins around Month 4 or 5. Some mothers leak regularly; others never leak at all during pregnancy. Both are completely normal. Absence of leaking does not indicate any problem and does not affect breastfeeding ability. If leaking is heavy or uncomfortable, breast pads placed inside your bra will help.
        What does colostrum look like during pregnancy?
        +
        Colostrum is typically thick, sticky, and yellow to orange in colour. This is entirely normal and is caused by its high concentration of beta-carotene and immune factors. As pregnancy progresses toward the third trimester, colostrum may become slightly clearer or more white in colour. If you notice any blood-tinged discharge from the nipple, contact your doctor.
        Can I collect colostrum before my baby is born?
        +
        Yes — antenatal colostrum harvesting is possible from around Week 36, with your doctor's approval. You gently hand-express colostrum into small sterile syringes and freeze them for use in your baby's first hours. This is particularly helpful for mothers whose babies may have difficulty feeding initially, or for those who want a stored supply ready. A <strong>Motherly <strong>lactation consultant</strong></strong> can guide you through this process safely. Book through the app at <strong><strong>mothrly.com</strong></strong>.
        Will breastfeeding an older child affect my pregnancy?
        +
        If you are pregnant and still breastfeeding an older child, small amounts of oxytocin released during nursing can cause mild uterine contractions. For most healthy pregnancies, this is not a concern. However, if you have a history of preterm labour, placenta praevia, or a high-risk pregnancy, your doctor may advise you to wean. Always discuss continued breastfeeding during pregnancy with your gynaecologist. Motherly's verified gynaecologists can provide personalised guidance through the app.
        Does stress during pregnancy affect breast milk production?
        +
        During pregnancy, colostrum production is largely hormonally driven and is not significantly impacted by stress. However, after birth, stress can suppress the let-down reflex and reduce milk supply by elevating cortisol — which suppresses prolactin and oxytocin. Managing postpartum stress is therefore a genuine breastfeeding strategy. <a href="https://mothrly.com/our-services/doulas/" target="_blank" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;"><strong>Motherly's doulas</strong></a> and <a href="https://mothrly.com/our-services/nannies-postnatal-care/" target="_blank" style="color:var(--red);font-weight:700;text-decoration:underline;"><strong>postnatal nannies</strong></a> are specifically trained to reduce postnatal stress and support milk production after birth.
        My friend's milk came in on Day 2 and mine came in on Day 5 — is that normal?
        +
        Completely normal. Mature milk typically comes in anywhere from Day 2 to Day 5. Factors that influence timing include whether this is your first or subsequent pregnancy, how frequently your baby feeds in the first 24 hours, your birth experience, and individual hormonal differences. If milk has not come in by Day 5, contact a <strong>Motherly <strong>lactation consultant</strong></strong> through the app for an assessment.
        How does Motherly support mothers with breastfeeding from pregnancy?
        +
        <strong>Motherly</strong> connects expecting mothers with <strong><strong>certified <strong>lactation consultant</strong></strong>s</strong> who can answer questions about when breast milk starts, guide you through antenatal colostrum harvesting from Week 36, help you prepare a breastfeeding plan before birth, and provide full support when mature milk arrives after delivery. Book directly through <strong>the <strong>Motherly app</strong></strong> — free on Android and iOS — or visit <strong><strong>mothrly.com</strong></strong>.
