  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2 id="toc-10">FAQs — How to Increase Breast Milk</h2>
        How quickly can I see results when trying to increase breast milk?
        +
        Most mothers notice an improvement in supply within 24–72 hours of increasing feed frequency or introducing galactagogue foods. Power pumping typically shows results within 3–5 days. If supply does not improve within a week of consistent effort, book a Motherly lactation consultant for a personalised assessment.
        Does drinking more water help to increase breast milk?
        +
        Yes — breast milk is over 80% water, so hydration is directly linked to milk production. Aim for 12–14 glasses of fluid daily. You should feel thirsty while breastfeeding — that is your body's signal. Keep a glass of water with you at every feed.
        Is fenugreek (methi) safe for increasing breast milk?
        +
        For most mothers, fenugreek is safe and effective. It is one of the most widely used galactagogues in Indian maternal care. However, if you have thyroid issues or diabetes, check with your doctor before using fenugreek. Some babies may become gassy — monitor and adjust if needed.
        Can stress reduce breast milk and how do I manage it?
        +
        Yes — cortisol (the stress hormone) directly suppresses prolactin, the hormone responsible for milk production. Managing stress is a genuine milk supply strategy. Rest when possible, accept help, and consider booking a Motherly postnatal care professional to support your recovery and reduce daily burden.
        How many times a day should I breastfeed to increase milk supply?
        +
        Aim for 8–12 feeds per 24 hours, including at least 1–2 night feeds when prolactin is highest. If your baby is not feeding frequently, add pumping sessions between feeds to maintain the demand signal. This is the foundation of every how to increase breast milk technique.
        Can I increase breast milk if my baby is mostly on formula?
        +
        Yes, in many cases it is possible to relactate or increase supply even if formula has been introduced. It requires consistent pumping, increasing time at the breast, and ideally professional support. Motherly's certified lactation consultants specialise in exactly this — book through the app at mothrly.com.
        What foods should I eat every day to increase breast milk?
        +
        A daily diet for increasing milk supply should include: oats or dalia at breakfast, dal (especially masoor or moong) at every meal, soaked almonds and walnuts, moringa leaves in cooking, garlic in daily cooking, and jeera or ajwain water throughout the day. Hydration with 12–14 glasses of fluid daily is equally important.
        How does Motherly help with breastfeeding and increasing breast milk?
        +
        Motherly connects you with certified lactation consultants who can assess your latch, identify supply issues, create a personalised plan, and provide ongoing support through the app. Book your session at mothrly.com or download the Motherly app — available free on Android and iOS.
